{"sourceSha256": "08608b9aa3e791aba54a625837f8b3a2abe68cf6076fd492750d3670a6d84843", "text": ": Pe ORT FEBRUARY 2015 °°\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Community Health Centre\nPATIENT You are 63 years old and in good health. You enjoy fresh air and sunshine. You\n\nrarely need a doctor. Six days ago when you were digging in the garden, a rusty\n| nail went through your rubber sandal and slightly penetrated the sole of your right\n| foot. You cleaned the wound with antiseptic and covered it with a sterile dressing.\nYesterday you read a newspaper article about people over 50 getting tetanus from\nsimple garden wounds.\n\nTASK e Be hesitant and apologetic (you do not usually ‘bother’ doctors). Explain the\n\nreason for your visit.\n\ne lf asked, you have no symptoms (only slight tenderness around the wound). Ask\nif tetanus is a serious condition. :\n\ne Find out if you need a tetanus vaccination. You had one when you were a child.\nWas the article exaggerating? Didn't the vaccination you had give permanent\nimmunisation?\nAsk if it's safe for you to continue gardening at your age.\n\ne Accept the doctor's suggestions.\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment\n\n(Gratin erie ee oe een OR TREBRUARY 2016 8s cE gee\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Community Health Centre\n\nDOCTOR This 63-year-old patient is enquiring about the need for a tetanus vaccination.\nHe/she cut his/her right foot while working in the garden recently. He/she cleaned\nthe wound but is worried about contracting tetanus.\n\nTASK e Find out the reasons for the patient's enquiry.\nH Check for any symptoms of tetanus ‘lockjaw’ (e.g., muscle stiffness and spasms\n| in the neck, especially a stiff jaw, 4-21 days after injury).\n‘ e Explain why tetanus is a serious disease (e.g., affects nervous system, muscles,\netc.).\n* Reassure the patient that the consultation was a good idea (booster required\nevery 10 years: immune system may not work as well with age). Recommend a\ntetanus vaccination if required (if previous vaccination was more than ten years\nago, advise the patient to have another booster).\n* Encourage the patient's gardening hobby (outdoor exercise).\ne Advise on suitable footwear to avoid future gardening injuries (strong, covering\nthe feet, thick soles, etc.}.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment FEBRUARY 2015", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": ": Pe ORT FEBRUARY 2015 °°\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Community Health Centre\nPATIENT You are 63 years old and in good health. You enjoy fresh air and sunshine. You\n\nrarely need a doctor. Six days ago when you were digging in the garden, a rusty\n| nail went through your rubber sandal and slightly penetrated the sole of your right\n| foot. You cleaned the wound with antiseptic and covered it with a sterile dressing.\nYesterday you read a newspaper article about people over 50 getting tetanus from\nsimple garden wounds.\n\nTASK e Be hesitant and apologetic (you do not usually ‘bother’ doctors). Explain the\n\nreason for your visit.\n\ne lf asked, you have no symptoms (only slight tenderness around the wound). Ask\nif tetanus is a serious condition. :\n\ne Find out if you need a tetanus vaccination. You had one when you were a child.\nWas the article exaggerating? Didn't the vaccination you had give permanent\nimmunisation?\nAsk if it's safe for you to continue gardening at your age.\n\ne Accept the doctor's suggestions.\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment\n\n(Gratin erie ee oe een OR TREBRUARY 2016 8s cE gee\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Community Health Centre\n\nDOCTOR This 63-year-old patient is enquiring about the need for a tetanus vaccination.\nHe/she cut his/her right foot while working in the garden recently. He/she cleaned\nthe wound but is worried about contracting tetanus.\n\nTASK e Find out the reasons for the patient's enquiry.\nH Check for any symptoms of tetanus ‘lockjaw’ (e.g., muscle stiffness and spasms\n| in the neck, especially a stiff jaw, 4-21 days after injury).\n‘ e Explain why tetanus is a serious disease (e.g., affects nervous system, muscles,\netc.).\n* Reassure the patient that the consultation was a good idea (booster required\nevery 10 years: immune system may not work as well with age). Recommend a\ntetanus vaccination if required (if previous vaccination was more than ten years\nago, advise the patient to have another booster).\n* Encourage the patient's gardening hobby (outdoor exercise).\ne Advise on suitable footwear to avoid future gardening injuries (strong, covering\nthe feet, thick soles, etc.}.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment FEBRUARY 2015", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 2163.0, "height": 2800.0, "blocks": [{"text": ": Pe ORT FEBRUARY 2015 °°\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE", "lines": [{"text": ": Pe ORT FEBRUARY 2015 °°", "x": 0.05871, "y": 0.0175, "width": 0.64216, "height": 0.02607, "confidence": 0.523, "words": [{"text": ":", "x": 0.05871, "width": 0.00601, "confidence": 0.43}, {"text": "Pe", "x": 0.25566, "width": 0.1165, "confidence": 0.258}, {"text": "ORT", "x": 0.411, "width": 0.04531, "confidence": 0.651}, {"text": "FEBRUARY", "x": 0.46001, "width": 0.18816, "confidence": 0.93}, {"text": "2015", "x": 0.59686, "width": 0.05086, "confidence": 0.867}, {"text": "°°", "x": 0.66251, "width": 0.03837, "confidence": 0.0}], "uncertainWords": [":", "Pe", "°°"]}, {"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE", "x": 0.04531, "y": 0.05, "width": 0.70596, "height": 0.0125, "confidence": 0.953, "words": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER", "x": 0.04531, "width": 0.15442, "confidence": 0.916}, {"text": "CARD", "x": 0.20804, "width": 0.0638, "confidence": 0.964}, {"text": "NO.", "x": 0.28294, "width": 0.03791, "confidence": 0.963}, {"text": "1", "x": 0.33056, "width": 0.00693, "confidence": 0.962}, {"text": "MEDICINE", "x": 0.64124, "width": 0.11003, "confidence": 0.961}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "SETTING Community Health Centre\nPATIENT You 